---
/*
  Privacy Policy — /privacy

  Astro turns this file into dist/privacy/index.html, which serves at
  https://offline.community/privacy/ (with the trailing slash). That exact
  URL is what's declared in the Dumb Down listing in Google Play Console,
  and Play requires it to be a live, non-editable, globally reachable page.

  ── Keeping this accurate ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  This describes the CURRENT Dumb Down Android app (com.offlineinc.dumbdown,
  v2.20.0 / versionCode 28). It was written against the app's manifest and
  source, not from memory. If the app's permissions or data flows change,
  this page and the Play "Data safety" form both need updating.

  Two claims below depend on backend behaviour, not app code — re-check them
  if the backend changes:
    1. "only your two phones hold the key" — assumes the pairing server
       discards the shared secret after issuing it. Note that
       /contact-sync/upload authenticates with an X-Auth-HMAC computed from
       that same secret, so the server must be verifying it some other way
       (e.g. a stored verifier) for this to hold.
    2. Retention windows in the "How long we keep things" section.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SectionHeader from '../components/SectionHeader.astro';

const lastUpdated = 'July 31, 2026';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy Policy — Dumb Down — Offline DC Inc"
  description="How the Dumb Down app handles contacts, phone numbers, and sign-in information."
  masthead="Month Offline > Privacy"
>
  <div class="sections">
    <SectionHeader label="Privacy policy" color="cyan" />

    <section class="card">
      <p class="body">
        <strong>Dumb Down</strong> &mdash; an app by <strong>OFFLINE DC INC</strong><br />
        <span class="meta">Last updated: {lastUpdated}</span>
      </p>

      <p class="body">
        Dumb Down is a companion app. It runs on your smartphone and helps you set
        up and live with a dumb phone: pairing the two devices, copying your
        contacts across, typing on the big phone so the words appear on the small
        one, and getting you signed in to the services your dumb phone uses.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        Everything below follows from that. We handle only what the setup
        actually needs, and we don't build a profile of you along the way.
      </p>

      <h3 class="label">The short version</h3>
      <p class="body">
        Dumb Down reads your contacts and relays sign-in information to your
        paired dumb phone, at your request. Anything that travels between your
        two phones is encrypted on the device it leaves from. We don't sell your
        data, we don't show ads, and the app contains no analytics, tracking, or
        crash-reporting SDKs. <strong>Dumb Down does not read, send, or store
        your text messages</strong>, and requests no SMS permission.
      </p>

      <h3 class="label">1. What the app handles</h3>

      <h4 class="sub">Contacts</h4>
      <p class="body">
        When you tap sync on the Contact Sync screen, the app reads the names and
        phone numbers in your contacts and packages them as a vCard file. That
        file is encrypted on your device with AES-256-GCM, using a key
        established when you paired your two phones, and then uploaded to our
        sync server so your dumb phone can collect it. Contacts saved on the dumb
        phone travel back to your smartphone the same way, and are added to your
        contacts list.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        Only the encrypted file reaches our server. Only your two paired phones
        hold the key needed to read it. Contact syncing runs when you ask it to
        &mdash; the app doesn't sync in the background &mdash; and your contacts
        are never used for advertising, analytics, or profiling.
      </p>

      <h4 class="sub">Phone numbers</h4>
      <p class="body">
        Setup needs both numbers: your smartphone's and your dumb phone's. Both
        are stored on your device. Your smartphone number is also sent to our
        server so we can set up and confirm text syncing and call forwarding.
        What we keep on our side is the phone number itself and a flag recording
        whether setup succeeded &mdash; not the contents of any message or call.
      </p>

      <h4 class="sub">Signing in to Google Messages</h4>
      <p class="body">
        Your dumb phone sends and receives texts through Google Messages, which
        means it needs to be signed in to your Google account. Dumb Down helps
        you do that in one of two ways: you sign in to Google inside the app, or
        you sign in on your computer and scan the resulting code with your
        phone's camera.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        Either way, the app takes the resulting Google session cookies, encrypts
        them on your device, and passes them through our relay to your paired
        dumb phone, which stores them and uses them to sign in. The relay hands
        the encrypted message straight to your dumb phone; we don't store these
        cookies on our servers, and we can't read them. We never ask for, see, or
        store your Google password.
      </p>

      <h4 class="sub">Typing on your dumb phone</h4>
      <p class="body">
        When you use your smartphone as a keyboard for your dumb phone, what you
        type is encrypted on your device and relayed to the paired phone. It
        isn't stored on our servers or retained after it arrives.
      </p>

      <h4 class="sub">Camera</h4>
      <p class="body">
        The camera is used for exactly one thing: scanning the sign-in code from
        your computer screen. No photo or video is saved, and nothing from the
        camera is uploaded.
      </p>

      <h4 class="sub">Pairing your devices</h4>
      <p class="body">
        To link the two phones, the app sends a randomly generated device
        identifier and the platform name ("android") to our server, which returns
        a short pairing code you enter on the dumb phone. This identifier isn't
        an advertising ID and isn't shared with anyone.
      </p>

      <h4 class="sub">Call forwarding</h4>
      <p class="body">
        The app works out the right carrier code for forwarding calls to your
        dumb phone and shows it to you. Dumb Down doesn't place calls itself and
        holds no call permission &mdash; the code goes to your phone's own dialer,
        where you place the call.
      </p>

      <h3 class="label">2. What the app does not do</h3>
      <p class="body">
        Dumb Down does not access SMS or MMS messages. It holds no SMS
        permission, reads no messages, sends no messages, and runs no
        notification listener. (Earlier versions of this policy described SMS
        forwarding; the app no longer works that way.)
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        It also does not: sell or rent personal data; share personal data with
        advertisers or data brokers; show ads; include analytics, attribution,
        tracking, or crash-reporting SDKs; access your location, photos,
        microphone, calendar, or files; or track you across other apps and
        websites.
      </p>

      <h3 class="label">3. Permissions the app asks for</h3>
      <p class="body">
        Android will prompt you for each of these, and you can refuse or revoke
        any of them in your device settings. Refusing one turns off the feature
        that needs it and nothing else.
      </p>
      <ul class="body list">
        <li><strong>Contacts (read and write)</strong> &mdash; to copy contacts to and from your dumb phone.</li>
        <li><strong>Camera</strong> &mdash; to scan the sign-in code from your computer.</li>
        <li><strong>Internet</strong> &mdash; to reach the pairing, sync, and relay services.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="label">4. Who else is involved</h3>
      <p class="body">
        We run our own backend on Heroku, a cloud hosting provider, which
        processes data on our behalf and under our instructions. When you sign in
        to your Google account, that exchange happens directly with Google and is
        governed by <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google's privacy policy</a>.
        We share personal data with no one else.
      </p>

      <h3 class="label">5. How long we keep things</h3>
      <ul class="body list">
        <li><strong>Encrypted contact files</strong> &mdash; held on the sync server only until your paired phone collects them, then replaced by the next sync.</li>
        <li><strong>Relayed messages</strong> (sign-in cookies, typed text) &mdash; passed straight through; not written to disk.</li>
        <li><strong>Phone numbers and setup status</strong> &mdash; kept while your devices are paired.</li>
        <li><strong>Data on your device</strong> &mdash; your numbers, chosen carrier, and pairing details live in the app's storage and are removed when you uninstall it or clear its data.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="label">6. Your choices</h3>
      <p class="body">
        You decide whether to sync contacts and whether to sign in. You can
        revoke any permission at any time in your device settings, unpair your
        phones from inside the app, or uninstall the app, which clears everything
        it stored locally.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        To ask what we hold about you, or to have it deleted from our servers,
        email <a href="mailto:team@offline.community">team@offline.community</a>.
        We'll respond within 30 days.
      </p>

      <h3 class="label">7. Children</h3>
      <p class="body">
        Dumb Down isn't directed at children under 13, and we don't knowingly
        collect personal information from them. If you believe a child under 13
        has given us personal information, email us and we'll delete it.
      </p>

      <h3 class="label">8. Changes to this policy</h3>
      <p class="body">
        If we change how the app handles information, we'll update this page and
        move the "last updated" date at the top. Material changes will also be
        noted in the app.
      </p>

      <h3 class="label">9. Contact us</h3>
      <p class="body">
        Questions about this policy, or about anything above:<br />
        <a href="mailto:team@offline.community">team@offline.community</a><br />
        OFFLINE DC INC, Washington, DC
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Standard body paragraph (Syne Mono) — same rhythm as the DIY pages. */
  .body {
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    line-height: var(--leading-body);
  }
  .body + .body {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }

  /* The "last updated" line, quieter than the copy around it. */
  .meta {
    font-size: var(--size-footer);
  }

  /* Sub-heading below a blue .label bar — plain text in the display face,
     for the individual data types inside section 1. */
  .sub {
    font-size: var(--size-body);
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
  }
  .sub + .body {
    margin-top: var(--space-1);
  }

  /* Bulleted lists sit inside the card's padding rather than hanging off it. */
  .list {
    margin: var(--space-2) 0 0;
    padding-left: var(--space-3);
  }
  .list li + li {
    margin-top: var(--space-1);
  }
</style>
